  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "The best overall roofing materials for Utah's climate are impact-resistant asphalt shingles for budget and versatility, or standing-seam metal roofing for long-term mountain durability.",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "impact-resistant asphalt shingles",
            "link": "https://roofingcenter.com/the-best-roofing-material-for-hail-resistance-in-the-mountain-west/"
          },
          {
            "text": "standing-seam metal roofing",
            "link": "https://utahroofing.com/services/roof-replacement/"
          }
        ],
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "impact-resistant asphalt shingles"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Utah features heavy winter snow, intense summer UV rays, high winds, and rapid temperature swings. Choosing the right material depends on your specific region, elevation, and budget."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Top Roofing Options for Utah"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Impact-Resistant Asphalt Shingles (Class 4):",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Pros: Most affordable, popular across the Wasatch Front, and handles hail and high winds well."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Cons: Shorter lifespan (typically 20 to 25 years) compared to metal or tile."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Best for: Standard suburban homes looking for a balance of cost and protection."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Standing-Seam Metal Roofing:",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Pros: Sheds heavy snow easily, resists fire, reflects intense UV rays, and lasts 40 to 50 years."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Cons: Higher upfront cost."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Best for: Mountain areas like Summit or Wasatch County with heavy snow loads."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Concrete and Clay Tile:",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Pros: Excellent against high heat and strong winds, highly reflective for cooling savings."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Cons: Very heavy; requires strong structural roof support and professional installation."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Best for: Southern Utah or lower elevations with intense sun exposure."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Factors to Consider"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Elevation and Snow: Mountain areas need smooth surfaces or snow retention systems to prevent dangerous ice and snow avalanches."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "UV and Temperature Swings: Utah's extreme heat and cold make basic shingles brittle faster. Look for high thermal emittance and solar reflectance."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Installation Quality: Proper ventilation, flashing, and underlayment matter just as much as the material itself."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you tell me your city or elevation in Utah and your budget, I can help you choose the exact right material and shingle class for your home."
      },
